From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Move the event queue links into the loop

The event loop's run queue was an intrusive doubly linked list through
the queued events, so arming an event wrote the links of its new
neighbors and unlinking one wrote the links of the neighbors it had.
An embedder that runs units of work under memory isolation, where a
unit may address only its own objects, cannot let a unit write a
neighbor another unit queued: with the events on one queue, every arm
and every disarm does.

The queue now runs through EventLink nodes the loop allocates, and an
armed event holds a pointer to the one standing in for it. Arming takes
a link off the loop's free list and links it in; disarming unlinks it
and returns it. Both write the loop's memory and the event's own `link`
field, and nothing of another event's. The loop's sentinels become
links too, and a link without an event is one of them (or one on the
free list), which is what a walk of the queue skips and what head()
now reports as an empty queue, in place of the tail sentinel.

The links come in chunks mapped from the OS, never moved or freed until
the loop is destroyed, since an event holds a link's address. They do
not come from the C++ heap because the embedder gives each unit of work
its own heap: a chunk allocated while a unit runs would land in that
unit's heap, out of every other unit's reach. Destroying the loop cuts
each still armed event loose from its link before unmapping, so that
destroying such an event afterwards writes nothing.
